1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,996
A maize field in the hot afternoon sun.

2
00:00:02,996 --> 00:00:05,427
A sugarcane patch, taller every week.

3
00:00:05,427 --> 00:00:07,527
Both love strong light and heat.

4
00:00:07,927 --> 00:00:10,453
Many other plants slow down in that heat.

5
00:00:10,453 --> 00:00:12,371
These two keep on making food.

6
00:00:12,821 --> 00:00:15,287
Aarav saw the maize
field from his bicycle.

7
00:00:15,287 --> 00:00:18,599
He asked: why do some plants
make more food in the heat?

8
00:00:18,599 --> 00:00:20,628
And what goes wrong in the others?

9
00:00:20,978 --> 00:00:22,800
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,308
These are C4 plants.

11
00:00:24,308 --> 00:00:28,882
They pump carbon dioxide into special
cells, so a wasteful process called

12
00:00:28,882 --> 00:00:30,886
photorespiration cannot happen.

13
00:00:31,386 --> 00:00:35,369
By the end, you will know the C4
pathway, Kranz anatomy, photorespiration,

14
00:00:35,369 --> 00:00:37,226
and how C3 and C4 plants differ.

15
00:00:37,876 --> 00:00:39,508
First, what do C3 and C4 mean?

16
00:00:39,858 --> 00:00:43,959
In the Calvin cycle, the first stable
product is PGA, a molecule with three

17
00:00:43,959 --> 00:00:44,859
carbon atoms.

18
00:00:44,823 --> 00:00:47,178
Plants that make PGA first are C3 plants.

19
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:51,873
In some plants, the first stable
product is oxaloacetic acid, or OAA.

20
00:00:51,873 --> 00:00:53,527
It has four carbon atoms.

21
00:00:53,527 --> 00:00:56,734
These are C4 plants, adapted
to dry tropical places.

22
00:00:57,384 --> 00:00:59,126
Let's zoom into a C4 leaf.

23
00:00:59,476 --> 00:01:00,877
Here is a maize leaf.

24
00:01:00,877 --> 00:01:04,211
Cut it across, and look at
the slice under a microscope.

25
00:01:04,711 --> 00:01:05,703
Zoom closer.

26
00:01:05,703 --> 00:01:09,384
Around each vascular bundle
sits a ring of large cells.

27
00:01:09,384 --> 00:01:11,746
These are the bundle sheath cells.

28
00:01:12,246 --> 00:01:13,627
Zoom into the ring.

29
00:01:13,627 --> 00:01:17,904
These cells have many chloroplasts,
thick walls that gases cannot pass,

30
00:01:17,904 --> 00:01:19,727
and no spaces between them.

31
00:01:19,727 --> 00:01:21,549
This ring is Kranz anatomy.

32
00:01:21,549 --> 00:01:22,872
Kranz means wreath.

33
00:01:23,522 --> 00:01:27,783
Now follow carbon dioxide through
the C4 pathway, step by step.

34
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:29,033
Step one.

35
00:01:29,016 --> 00:01:34,098
In a mesophyll cell, carbon dioxide
joins PEP, a three carbon molecule.

36
00:01:34,098 --> 00:01:37,189
The enzyme is PEP carboxylase, or PEPcase.

37
00:01:37,939 --> 00:01:38,839
Step two.

38
00:01:38,678 --> 00:01:41,040
This makes OAA, the four carbon acid.

39
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:45,395
It changes into other four carbon
acids, like malic acid or aspartic acid.

40
00:01:46,145 --> 00:01:47,045
Step three.

41
00:01:46,847 --> 00:01:49,236
These acids travel into
the bundle sheath cells.

42
00:01:49,986 --> 00:01:50,886
Step four.

43
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:52,641
There they are broken down.

44
00:01:52,641 --> 00:01:56,081
They release carbon dioxide
and a three carbon molecule.

45
00:01:56,831 --> 00:01:57,731
Step five.

46
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:01,265
The carbon dioxide enters the
Calvin cycle, run by RuBisCO.

47
00:02:01,265 --> 00:02:05,709
The three carbon molecule goes back to
the mesophyll and becomes PEP again.

48
00:02:06,459 --> 00:02:07,427
Remember this.

49
00:02:07,427 --> 00:02:10,540
C4 plants pump carbon dioxide
into the bundle sheath.

50
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:12,095
Why pump it?

51
00:02:12,095 --> 00:02:14,043
Because RuBisCO has a weakness.

52
00:02:14,393 --> 00:02:18,211
The active site of RuBisCO can
bind carbon dioxide or oxygen.

53
00:02:18,211 --> 00:02:21,895
Which one it grabs depends on
how much of each is around.

54
00:02:22,345 --> 00:02:26,542
When oxygen binds instead, RuBP splits
into one PGA and one phosphoglycolate,

55
00:02:26,542 --> 00:02:27,926
which has two carbons.

56
00:02:27,926 --> 00:02:29,389
This is photorespiration.

57
00:02:30,039 --> 00:02:32,058
Photorespiration is a waste.

58
00:02:32,058 --> 00:02:33,158
Here is why.

59
00:02:33,508 --> 00:02:35,449
It makes no sugar and no ATP.

60
00:02:35,449 --> 00:02:38,836
Instead, it uses up ATP and
lets carbon dioxide escape.

61
00:02:39,286 --> 00:02:43,515
In C4 plants, the bundle sheath
is packed with carbon dioxide.

62
00:02:43,515 --> 00:02:48,094
So RuBisCO grabs carbon dioxide, and
photorespiration does not happen.

63
00:02:48,094 --> 00:02:50,954
That is why C4 plants give better yields.

64
00:02:51,604 --> 00:02:53,981
Let's put C3 and C4 plants side by side.

65
00:02:54,331 --> 00:02:58,877
C3 plants, like wheat, fix carbon
dioxide once, with RuBisCO in mesophyll

66
00:02:58,877 --> 00:02:59,777
cells.

67
00:02:59,465 --> 00:03:01,294
Their first product is PGA.

68
00:03:01,294 --> 00:03:03,567
Their leaves have no Kranz anatomy.

69
00:03:04,017 --> 00:03:06,994
C4 plants, like maize and
sorghum, fix it twice.

70
00:03:06,994 --> 00:03:10,778
First PEPcase in the mesophyll,
then RuBisCO in the bundle sheath.

71
00:03:10,778 --> 00:03:12,447
Their first product is OAA.

72
00:03:13,047 --> 00:03:13,947
Your turn.

73
00:03:13,850 --> 00:03:17,643
Does photorespiration happen in
the bundle sheath of a C4 plant?

74
00:03:17,643 --> 00:03:18,824
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:22,024 --> 00:03:23,623
No, it does not!

76
00:03:23,623 --> 00:03:28,822
So much carbon dioxide is packed there
that RuBisCO grabs carbon dioxide,

77
00:03:28,822 --> 00:03:29,880
not oxygen.

78
00:03:30,530 --> 00:03:32,080
Now, a worked example.

79
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,061
A leaf slice shows three clues.

80
00:03:34,061 --> 00:03:35,253
Is it C3 or C4?

81
00:03:35,603 --> 00:03:36,503
Clue one.

82
00:03:36,395 --> 00:03:39,668
Large cells form a ring
around each vascular bundle.

83
00:03:40,118 --> 00:03:41,018
Clue two.

84
00:03:40,888 --> 00:03:44,603
RuBisCO is found in those ring
cells, not in the mesophyll.

85
00:03:45,053 --> 00:03:45,953
Clue three.

86
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:48,966
The first stable product
is a four carbon acid.

87
00:03:48,966 --> 00:03:50,498
All three clues agree.

88
00:03:50,498 --> 00:03:51,799
It is a C4 leaf.

89
00:03:52,449 --> 00:03:53,910
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:57,308
Many students think C4 plants do
not use the Calvin cycle at all.

91
00:03:57,708 --> 00:03:58,616
But they do.

92
00:03:58,616 --> 00:04:01,560
The Calvin cycle runs in
their bundle sheath cells.

93
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:04,371
The C4 pathway only brings
carbon dioxide to it.

94
00:04:05,021 --> 00:04:06,275
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:06,275 --> 00:04:10,027
As match and statement questions on
Kranz anatomy, PEPcase, RuBisCO and

96
00:04:10,027 --> 00:04:11,003
photorespiration.

97
00:04:11,603 --> 00:04:12,779
Exam level now.

98
00:04:12,779 --> 00:04:15,452
Match each item with its place or role.

99
00:04:15,452 --> 00:04:16,678
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:22,078 --> 00:04:23,326
The answer is C.

101
00:04:23,326 --> 00:04:25,453
PEPcase works in mesophyll cells.

102
00:04:25,453 --> 00:04:28,887
In a C4 plant, RuBisCO works
in bundle sheath cells.

103
00:04:29,387 --> 00:04:31,407
Phosphoglycolate is made
in photorespiration.

104
00:04:31,907 --> 00:04:34,737
And OAA is the first stable
product of the C4 pathway.

105
00:04:35,337 --> 00:04:36,878
Here is your recap.

106
00:04:36,878 --> 00:04:38,462
Screenshot this card.

107
00:04:41,662 --> 00:04:43,848
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

108
00:04:44,248 --> 00:04:45,148
One.

109
00:04:44,633 --> 00:04:45,833
What is Kranz anatomy?

110
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:47,633
Two.

111
00:04:47,224 --> 00:04:51,183
Which enzyme fixes carbon dioxide
in the mesophyll of C4 plants?

112
00:04:52,083 --> 00:04:52,983
Three.

113
00:04:52,642 --> 00:04:54,615
Why is photorespiration a waste?

114
00:04:55,715 --> 00:05:00,223
Next time: why does a plant stop
speeding up, even when you give it more

115
00:05:00,223 --> 00:05:01,123
light?

116
00:05:00,802 --> 00:05:03,715
We find out in factors
affecting photosynthesis.
